{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Do not profane your daughter, to make her a harlot</b> — by handing over his daughter when she isstill ה [unbetrothed] not for the purpose of kiddushin [betrothal]. <b>And the land shall not fall to harlotry</b> — if you do so, the land prostitutes its fruits, causing them to grow in another place and not in your land. And likewise Scripture says (Jeremiah 3:3), \"Therefore the showers have been withheld, and the latter rain has not come; yet you had the forehead of a harlotous woman,\" etc. — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:29). But I do not understand his opinion, for there is no zenut [harlotry/prostitution] in the Torah in the case of a penuya [unmarried woman], since it is an established halakhah (Yevamot 61a) that an unmarried man who lies with a penuya not for the purpose of ishus [matrimony] does not make her a zonah [harlot]. And it is explicitly stated in Sanhedrin (50b) concerning \"And the daughter of a priest, when she begins to play the harlot\" (Leviticus 21:9) — one might think even if she is a penuya, and they raised the difficulty: but \"to play the harlot\" is written [implying actual zenut], and they resolved it: according to Rabbi Eliezer, who says that an unmarried man who lies with a penuya not for the purpose of ishus makes her a zonah. Similarly, they said concerning \"A zonah or a halalah they shall not take\" (Leviticus 21:7) — this does not apply to a penuya, but rather to a convert or a freed maidservant, or one who has engaged in an act of zenut [bî'at zenut] with a man concerning whom kiddushin cannot take effect with her, as is explained in Yevamot (ibid.). And in the Sifra (Emor, chapter 1:7): \"A woman who is a zonah\" — Rabbi Yehudah says this is an aylonit [a woman incapable of bearing children]; but the Sages say: a zonah is none other than a convert and a freed maidservant and one who has engaged in an act of zenut; Rabbi Eliezer says: even an unmarried man who lies with a penuya not for the purpose of ishus. And thus they said (Temurah 29b) regarding \"You shall not bring the hire of a zonah\" (Deuteronomy 23:19) — this does not apply to a penuya at all.\n\nHowever, the other baraita that is cited in Torat Kohanim (chapter 7:1) and is referred to in tractate Sanhedrin (76a) — either it follows Rabbi Eliezer and is not the halakhah, or it refers to one who hands over his daughter to a man concerning whom there is no ishus [legal marital bond], such as a Cuthean [Samaritan], a slave, or those liable to karet [excision] or death penalties at the hands of the court, for this is called zenut according to the Sages. And this is what they said: \"one who hands over his daughter, a penuya, not for the purpose of ishus\" — that is, to a man between whom and her it is impossible for there to be ishus. Similarly, what we learned there in the Sifra (ibid. 7:2), \"And the land shall not fall to harlotry\" — \"the fruits are prostituted\" — this is a midrashic interpretation [derash], derived from the word \"the land,\" since it is not written \"and the men of the land shall not prostitute themselves.\" But the essential meaning of the verse is that it constitutes a second negative commandment [lav sheni], and the Ba'al Halakhot Gedolot already counted it in his reckoning of the negative commandments. It is a warning to the man who commits zenut and to the daughter herself, that she should not surrender herself for the purpose of zenut. And from here is what they said there in the baraita (Torat Kohanim, ibid. 7:1): \"and likewise it applies to her who surrenders herself not for the purpose of ishus.\"\n\nAnd the reason of the verse is that because the daughter is in the domain [reshut] of her father, and he has the authority during her youth to betroth her and bring her into the huppah [marriage canopy], and one who coerces or seduces her gives her fine [knas] to the father, Scripture explained that he is not permitted to hand her over to anyone in a manner of zenut, and it warned him against this by a negative commandment. And after the warning addressed to the father, it also warned those who commit zenut themselves — the man and the woman.\n\nAnd Rashi wrote in Parashat Emor el HaKohanim (Leviticus 21:7): \"Zonah\" — one who has engaged in an act of zenut with an Israelite man who is forbidden to her, such as one liable to karet or a natin [temple slave descendant] or a mamzer. And this is an admission on his part that there is no zenut among those who are fit [ksharim] for each other, except that he extends it to include even those liable to negative commandments [ḥayyavei lavim], and this is his opinion also in his commentaries on the Gemara (Yevamot 61a). But even this is not correct: rather, from those liable to karet and from a Cuthean and a slave — concerning whom kiddushin cannot take effect with her — she becomes a zonah, and a kohen [priest] receives lashes for her on account of zonah. But from those liable to negative commandments — a mamzer, a convert, an Ammonite and a Moabite — she is not a zonah, and he does not receive lashes for her; rather, she is disqualified from terumah [priestly portions] and from the priesthood, as it is written (Leviticus 22:12), \"If she becomes the wife of a stranger\" — once she has engaged in relations with one who is a pasul [disqualified person] to her, she is disqualified (Yevamot 68b), and through one liable to a negative commandment within the priesthood she becomes a halalah [a woman of blemished priestly status]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The acronym ת\"כ (Torat Kohanim) and the alternate name Sifra refer to the same halakhic midrash on Leviticus; both are preserved as Ramban cites them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:09.876540666Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}